The three primary mechanisms employed by network tarpits are:
1) a means to determine which IP addresses on a subnetwork are unused and thus may be faked;
2) a strategy to impersonate hosts by responding to TCP, UDP, or ICMP probes destined to fake IP addresses;
3) a method to hold the TCP connection open.

Classical Hebbian learning updates synaptic weights based on the coincident activity of pre- and post-synaptic neurons, as given by the formula: ∆wi j = η · xi · yj. Spike-Time-Dependent Plasticity (STDP) takes spike timing into account by adjusting weights based on the time difference between pre- and post-synaptic spikes, following the rule: Δ𝑤=
\[
\begin{cases}
𝜂+ 𝑒^{-|Δ𝑡|/𝜏+} & \text{if } Δ𝑡> 0 \\
𝜂-𝑒^{-|Δ𝑡|/𝜏-} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
